[[File:Prefectures of Nakamizu.png|250px|thumb|right|Nakamizu Administration Division since 1902 showing 40 Prefectures inside 7 regions]]
Nakahara started to use the prefecture system since the abolition of [[wikipedia:Han system|han system]] in 1820. Initially the prefectures was directly derived from the existing domains on the [[Nagashima (Nakamizu)|Nagashima]], [[Nankaido]] and [[Suzuya]] islands and its surrounding administered under the [[Kawaei Shogunate]]. Total 58 Prefectures emerged in the initial formation, in which later reduced into 38 by 1870. In 1902, the Nankaido prefecture was split into [[Rumoi Prefecture|Rumoi]], [[Kushiro Prefecture|Kushiro]] and [[Tokushiri Prefecture|Tokushiri]]. In 1945 Katajima and Aoyama elevated its status into metropolis prefecture with each own special wards to allow better planning. 

Prefix 'ken' (県) are used for most prefecture in Nakamizu with exception in some case such as [[Katajima]], and [[Aoyama]] which is a Special Metropolis Prefecture use 'to' (都) and [[Miyagami Prefecture|Miyagami]] use 'fu' (府). With the special metropolis prefecture, Katajima and Aoyama have special wards which acting like a city instead of normal wards in Designated cities.

